<section class="section section--flush">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The constraint I set myself</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>Ship zero JavaScript, and give up nothing for it.</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        That constraint is not asceticism. On a site whose purpose is
        demonstrating that I understand crawlability and performance, every
        kilobyte of script is a claim I have to defend. Anyone evaluating me can
        press Ctrl+U, open DevTools, and check whether the architecture matches
        the argument. So the rule was: if the platform can do it, the platform
        does it.
      </p>

      <h2>Static-first, and that's the whole stack</h2>

      <p>
        Every page here is a complete, pre-rendered HTML document at a real URL,
        generated at build time by Eleventy. There is no server-side rendering
        at request time, no client-side routing, no hydration, and no runtime
        framework. What the crawler receives is what the browser renders.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Only one representation of each page exists.</strong> There
          are no fragment or partial URLs, so there is nothing extra to crawl,
          canonicalize, or de-duplicate. The duplicate-content surface is zero.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Content has no rendering dependency.</strong> Everything is in
          the first response. No JS execution is required to see any of it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Nothing degrades.</strong> There is no enhanced mode to fall
          out of, because there is no enhancement layer to begin with.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Instant navigation without JavaScript</h2>

      <p>
        The usual reason to add a router is to avoid the flash between pages.
        I wanted that too, so I delegated it to the browser using the
        <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Speculation_Rules_API" rel="noopener">Speculation&nbsp;Rules&nbsp;API</a>
        &mdash; a small block of declarative JSON, not a script:
      </p>

      <pre><code>&lt;script type="speculationrules"&gt;
{
  "prerender": [
    {
      "where": { "href_matches": "/*" },
      "eagerness": "moderate"
    }
  ]
}
&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>

      <p>
        On a supporting browser, hovering or pressing down on a same-origin link
        causes the next page to be fetched <em>and fully rendered</em> in the
        background. By the time the click registers, the document is already
        painted, so the navigation is effectively instant &mdash; faster than a
        client-side router, which can only begin fetching <em>after</em> the
        click.
      </p>

      <p>
        The part I care about most is what this <em>doesn't</em> require. Because
        every transition is a genuine navigation, the browser handles focus
        placement, scroll restoration, history entries, the document
        <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>, the canonical link, and the screen reader's
        page-change announcement. All of it is correct by default.
      </p>

      <h2>The accessibility bug I removed by deleting code</h2>

      <p>
        This site could've easily used a JavaScript library to swap page content
        without a full navigation. That approach works, but it silently breaks
        things the browser otherwise gets right: screen readers are not told the
        page changed, and focus is left wherever it was &mdash; often at the
        bottom of the previous page.
      </p>

      <p>
        Repairing that took more tooling or a hand-written script: an
        <code>aria-live</code> region to announce each new page title, code to
        move focus to <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, code to reset scroll position,
        logic to parse the incoming response and sync the
        <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>, meta description, canonical, and robots
        directive, and a fallback path for failed requests.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is roughly 120 lines of code whose entire purpose is to make a
        simulated navigation behave like a real one &mdash; plus a second,
        untested copy of metadata logic that already exists in the template,
        free to drift out of sync. Avoiding a  library deleted the bug class
        along with it. <strong>The most reliable accessibility fix available
        here was removing the thing that required the fix.</strong>
      </p>

      <h2>Why there is no framework here</h2>

      <p>
        I measured rather than assumed. A library I could've used and the repair script came to roughly 55&nbsp;KB uncompressed. The entire site &mdash; fifteen pages of HTML &mdash; averages about 15&nbsp;KB per page.
      </p>

      <p>
        So a first-time visitor downloaded more JavaScript than they would read
        content across three pages, in order to avoid a transition flash that
        modern browsers already suppress through paint holding. The script also
        competed directly against the performance and blocking-time budgets this
        repository enforces in CI. The cost was real and recurring; the benefit
        was marginal and already partly provided for free.
      </p>

      <p>
        Avoiding it was not a downgrade. It was faster navigation, better
        accessibility, and less code.
      </p>

      <h2>The accessibility work that remains</h2>

      <p>
        Avoiding the navigation layer prevents the hardest accessibility problem
        on this site, but not the responsibility. What remains is deliberate:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          A skip link, semantic landmarks, one <code>h1</code> per page, and a
          heading hierarchy that never skips a level.
        </li>
        <li>
          Visible focus indicators, and a <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> element with
          <code>tabindex="-1"</code> so the skip link can move focus into it.
        </li>
        <li>WCAG&nbsp;2.2&nbsp;AA contrast, verified in CI rather than by eye.</li>
        <li>
          <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> and
          <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> both honoured.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Performance decisions</h2>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Decision</th><th>Reason</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>System font stack</td>
            <td>Zero font requests, zero FOUT, no layout shift from font swap.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Single stylesheet, no framework</td>
            <td>No unused CSS to ship or purge.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>AVIF and WebP with JPEG fallback</td>
            <td>The headshot is 15&nbsp;KB as AVIF versus 30&nbsp;KB as JPEG.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Explicit width and height on images</td>
            <td>Reserves layout space, which protects CLS.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>fetchpriority="high"</code> on the hero image</td>
            <td>It is the LCP element on the pages where it appears.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>No JavaScript</td>
            <td>Nothing to download, parse, execute, block rendering, or keep accessible.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>No analytics, no third-party scripts</td>
            <td>Nothing to slow the page, and nothing to disclose about visitors.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>SEO implementation</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>Unique title and meta description on every page, generated from front matter so they can't silently drift.</li>
        <li>Self-referencing canonical on every page.</li>
        <li>JSON-LD graph: <code>Person</code>, <code>WebSite</code>, <code>ProfilePage</code> on the about page, <code>Article</code> on case studies, and <code>BreadcrumbList</code> throughout.</li>
        <li>Open Graph and Twitter card metadata with a generated 1200&times;630 image.</li>
        <li><code>sitemap.xml</code> generated from the content collection, so a new page cannot be omitted by forgetting to update it.</li>
        <li>Content delivered in the initial HTML response, with no rendering step between the crawler and the text.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>CI, and why it's the interesting part</h2>

      <p>
        Claiming a site is well-optimized is weak evidence. Enforcing it
        mechanically is stronger. Every pull request to this repository runs:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>html-validate</strong> &mdash; markup validity.</li>
        <li><strong>Metadata uniqueness check</strong> &mdash; a script that fails the build if any page is missing a title, description, or canonical, or if any two pages share one.</li>
        <li><strong>lychee</strong> &mdash; internal and external link checking.</li>
        <li><strong>Lighthouse CI</strong> &mdash; with assertions requiring a perfect SEO score and a perfect accessibility score. The build fails if either regresses.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        That last one is the point. The quality claim on this site isn't a claim;
        it's a gate. I can't merge a change that breaks it without the pipeline
        telling me.
      </p>

      <h2>Hosting, and an honest trade-off</h2>

      <p>
        The site is hosted on GitHub Pages and deployed from <code>main</code>
        by GitHub Actions. Nothing is uploaded by hand; the artifact that passes
        the gates above is the artifact that ships.
      </p>

      <p>
        The real limitation of this host is that it cannot set custom response
        headers. HSTS, a content security policy, and
        <code>X-Content-Type-Options</code> are therefore unavailable. Moving to
        a platform that supports them would mean taking on DNS and operational
        risk for a site that does not carry the kind of traffic those headers
        protect, so I accepted the limitation instead.
      </p>

      <p>
        What remains is a <code>referrer</code> meta tag, plus the structural
        reasons those headers matter less here than they would elsewhere: no
        forms, no authentication, no cookies, no user input, no third-party
        scripts, and now no JavaScript at all. A content security policy
        mitigates injection; this site has no injection surface. HSTS protects
        credentials in transit; there are no credentials.
      </p>

      <p>
        I'm documenting the trade-off rather than quietly dropping the claim,
        because deciding which best practice to spend risk on is most of the
        job. If this site handled user input, I'd have made the opposite call.
      </p>

      <h2>What I'd do differently at scale</h2>

      <p>
        For a site this size, the architecture above is close to optimal. At a
        thousand pages I'd tune the prerender <code>eagerness</code> down to
        avoid speculating on links nobody follows, add incremental builds so a
        one-page change doesn't rebuild the world, and move to a real image CDN
        rather than generating every derivative at build time.
      </p>

      <p>
        I'd also expect the zero-JavaScript rule to stop holding. A search
        interface, a filterable index, or anything with genuine state is worth
        shipping code for. The rule was never "JavaScript is bad" &mdash; it was
        "make it justify its weight", and on fifteen static pages it couldn't.
        Knowing where an approach stops scaling is part of knowing the approach.
      </p>
